const body = `<h1>Writing a BankAccount Class — Full Walkthrough</h1>

<p>
    This is the program your teacher builds live.
    Unlike the immutability of Card, BankAccount has state that changes:
    deposits, withdrawals, interest. This is where mutators (setters) shine.
    Use this as a reference when building your own classes.
</p>

<h2>The Goal</h2>

<p>Create a BankAccount class with balance tracking, deposits, withdrawals with a minimum-balance guard, and interest calculation.</p>

<pre><code><span class="output">Created account #1001 for Alice with $500.00</span>
<span class="output">Deposited $200.00 — new balance: $700.00</span>
<span class="output">Withdrew $150.00 — new balance: $550.00</span>
<span class="output">Withdrew $600.00? Insufficient funds!</span>
<span class="output">After 5% interest: $577.50</span></code></pre>

<h2>Step 1: Instance Variables + Constructor</h2>

<pre><code>public class BankAccount {
    private int accountNumber;
    private String ownerName;
    private double balance;
    private static int nextAccountNumber = 1001;

    public BankAccount(String ownerName, double initialDeposit) {
        this.ownerName = ownerName;
        this.balance = initialDeposit;
        this.accountNumber = nextAccountNumber;
        nextAccountNumber++;  // auto-generate unique account numbers
    }</code></pre>

<p>
    <strong>Static counter pattern.</strong> nextAccountNumber belongs to the class,
    not individual objects. Every new account gets an incremented number.
    Same pattern as Monster's defeatedCount.
</p>

<h2>Step 2: Getters + Display</h2>

<pre><code>    public int getAccountNumber() { return accountNumber; }
    public String getOwnerName()  { return ownerName; }
    public double getBalance()    { return balance; }

    public String toString() {
        return "Account #" + accountNumber + " (" + ownerName
               + "): $" + String.format("%.2f", balance);
    }</code></pre>

<p>
    <strong>String.format("%.2f", balance)</strong> rounds to two decimal places.
    Without it, you might see $577.5000000000001. With it: $577.50. Clean money.
</p>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>String.format is not on the exam sheet.</strong> The
    <a href="../../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> gives you no
    formatting method at all, so the sheet's answer is concatenation with the padding
    built by hand. That is every use in this walkthrough: the two
    <code>toString</code> lines and the two <code>println</code> lines in deposit and
    withdraw.
<pre><code>int cents = (int)(balance * 100 + 0.5);   // round to the nearest cent
String money = "$" + (cents / 100) + "."
             + (cents % 100 &lt; 10 ? "0" : "") + (cents % 100);</code></pre>
    <p>57750 cents becomes <code>"$577.50"</code>, and the <code>"0"</code> branch is what
    keeps 5 cents from printing as <code>".5"</code>. The <code>+ 0.5</code> handles
    rounding for the positive balances the guards above allow. Keep
    <code>String.format</code> in the class below, it is the right tool in your own
    programs, and know that on paper you would write the lines above instead.</p>
</div>

<h2>Step 3: Mutators — Deposit and Withdraw</h2>

<pre><code>    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Deposit must be positive.");
            return;   // exit early — do nothing
        }
        balance += amount;
        System.out.println("Deposited $" + String.format("%.2f", amount));
    }

    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Withdrawal must be positive.");
            return false;
        }
        if (amount > balance) {
            System.out.println("Insufficient funds!");
            return false;
        }
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrew $" + String.format("%.2f", amount));
        return true;  // success
    }</code></pre>

<p>
    <strong>Guard clauses.</strong> Check for bad input FIRST and bail out.
    This keeps the happy path (the actual withdrawal logic) clean and un-nested.
    Returning boolean lets the caller check if the operation succeeded.
</p>

<h2>Step 4: Interest (Another Mutator)</h2>

<pre><code>    public void applyInterest(double rate) {
        balance += balance * (rate / 100.0);  // rate is a percentage
    }
}</code></pre>

<h2>The Full Class + Test</h2>

<pre><code>public class BankAccount {
    private int accountNumber;
    private String ownerName;
    private double balance;
    private static int nextAccountNumber = 1001;

    public BankAccount(String ownerName, double initialDeposit) {
        this.ownerName = ownerName;
        this.balance = initialDeposit;
        this.accountNumber = nextAccountNumber++;
    }

    public int getAccountNumber() { return accountNumber; }
    public String getOwnerName()  { return ownerName; }
    public double getBalance()    { return balance; }

    public String toString() {
        return "Account #" + accountNumber + " (" + ownerName
               + "): $" + String.format("%.2f", balance);
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Deposit must be positive.");
            return;
        }
        balance += amount;
    }

    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance) return false;
        balance -= amount;
        return true;
    }

    public void applyInterest(double rate) {
        balance += balance * (rate / 100.0);
    }
}

// Test:
public class BankTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount alice = new BankAccount("Alice", 500.0);
        System.out.println(alice);

        alice.deposit(200);
        alice.withdraw(150);
        alice.applyInterest(5.0);
        System.out.println(alice);
    }
}</code></pre>

<h2>Extending the Program</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Add a transfer(BankAccount destination, double amount) method.</li>
    <li>Add a transaction history — every deposit/withdrawal recorded.</li>
    <li>Add minimum-balance fees if balance drops below $100.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How This Connects to Your RPG</h2>

<p>
    BankAccount is the closest match to Player's HP management: setHP, takeDamage, heal.
    The guard-clause pattern (check for bad input, bail early) is identical.
    The static counter maps to Monster's defeatedCount.
</p>

